Date: Wed, 30 Jun 93 11:20:02 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: NanoFAQ

After a little over a week of operation, we've made some interesting
progress in our virtual seminar on "Algorithmic Information Theory."
Perhaps too much progress, as several people have only recently gotten
ahold of the "Level 0" reading material!

Maybe this "NanoFAQ" (much smaller than even a MicroFAQ) can reorient
readers and participants who may be disoriented by the sometimes dizzying
concepts. Or reassure those who think this AIT stuff is overrated.

Here goes:


1. What is AIT?

Classical information theory, largely the result of Claude Shannon, has a
primary focus on communications, bandwidth, signal-to-noise ratios, and how
to encode a message so that it gets through even in the presence of noise.
This was the key issue in the 1940s, and remains important today (with
thousands of radio and television channels, lots of background noise, fiber
optics, etc.)

A good intro to classical information theory is John R. Pierce's "Symbols,
Signals, and Noise." A much more advanced treatment is "Elements of
Information Theory," by Cover and Thomas.

In the 1960s, several independent researchers recast classical information
theory in terms of more basic computer science terms, such as complexity of
strings and the basic "length of a program." The great Russian
mathematician Kolmogorov, an American industry mathematician Ray
Solomonoff, and the college student Gregory Chaitin independently came up
with the same formulation: that the information content (or complexity,
loosely speaking) of an object depends on the size of a program needed to
reproduce the object.

Thus, the string "101010101010101.....10101010" is easily describable as
"alternating 1s and 0s," while the string "11001001101010100001....111101"
may have no shorter description than itself.

This, in a nutshell, is algorithmic information theory.


2. Who cares how long a program is?

Program length to generate an object is of practical significance. And
biological evolution clearly has to deal with this, as the genome must
carry all its own instructions, so string complexity has direct bearing on
the capabilities of a biological organism.

Randomness is also an important issue, in cryptography and in simulations
of systems (Monte Carlo methods need strong random numbers). AIT offers a
more succinct look at random numbers (or suspected random numbers,
pseudorandom numbers, etc.).


3. Can the shortest description of an object be found?

Not in general, as this has been shown to be undecideable. Finding shorter
descriptions of some object (like a string) is closely related to issues of
cleverness (IQ tests often rely on ability to see patterns in seemingly
random sequences).

The shortest description is also related to compression, of course.


4. What does AIT have to do with evolution, life, and Extropian interests?

First of all, it is not some all-encompassing theory that answers all
questions. It's just one way of looking at things. (Just as "information
theory" is often misapplied to life, art, music, politics, etc., so, too,
can algorithmic information theory be misapplied by enthusiasts.)

AIT appears to be a powerful tool for measuring the complexity of evolved
organisms, even if we cannot in practice compute the "minimal description."
The algorithmic comlexity of genomes is one obvious area, as are the schema
complexities in genetic algorithms and genetic programming. (Several recent
postings by some of us have dealt with this.)

I also feel it has a lot to do with the complexity of self-reproducing
systems, such as envisioned by nanotechnology enthusiasts.


5. Is there a future synthesis coming?

Clearly. If Shannon's classical information theory has been augmented with
algorithmic information theory, what's coming next?

The "logical depth" ideas of Bennett, Wolfram, Chaitin, and others,
suggests a space-time diagram of tradeoffs in complexity. Some objects
appear to be the "output" of a program (of whatever length) that has "run"
for a very long time. Life is the obvious example of this, cellular
automata are another.

(The much-ballyhooed "Mandelbrot set" has very low algorithmic information
content, in the sense than a few bits completely specifies the parameters
of the set, but any given approximation to the Mandelbrot set--such as one
might see in a photograph--may be the result of several hours of running on
a powerful workstation computer, implying great logical depth.)

Adding "fitness" for survival and reproduction may lead to a kind of
"evolutionary information theory."

(Some quibble that this brings in the external environment--the geographic
landscape, the ecological niches, competitors, etc.--and hence the measure
of an object/lifeform/string is dependent on more than just itself. Ah, but
information measures have _always_ depended on such things as chosen
alphabets, on the machines on which programs are run, and on the "context."
To put it bluntly, there is no single measure of the complexity of an
isolated object....it depends on context, on the embedding of an object in
some environment.)


6. What's the "Omega" stuff all about?

Chaitin used algorithmic information theory to recast the Godel and Turing
proofs of incompleteness/incomputability in information theory terms. He
showed that a "10 pound set of axioms" can't prove a "20 pound theory," as
he puts it. That is, a finite set of axioms does not carry enough
information to prove all theorems.

In particular, Chaitin has shown that with a given set of axioms, there
will be theorems whose truth or falsity appears completely random to us.
(This statement frequently causes consternation, guffaws, confusion, and
flaming!)

(There is much more to be said here. See the Rudy Rucker explanation in
"Mind Tools," 1987, or various other explanations. Chaitin's books are less
readable, but are of course the primary sources.)

Chaitin has invented the "Omega" number, which essentially encodes the
answers to all formal problems in a number between 0 and 1, a probability
that a Turing Machine (a model of a general computer) will halt when
presented with various inputs. 

It's not clear what this means for ordinary affairs, except that it
captures some sense of how much is "out there" awaiting eventual discover
(or maybe not). Chaitin argues, persuasively I think, that mathematics will
likely become more and more of an "experimental science." (Interestingly,
the recent apparent proof of Fermat's Last Theorem" made no use of
computers.)

Life is, as Chaitin puts it, the largest and longest-running "computation"
we have ever seen or likely ever will see. In a sense, then, life is
"approximating" Omega (this is potentially misleading: biology is happening
on a wide variety of "Turing Machines," and with wildly varying inputs.
Whether anything about Omega can be deduced from observation of life is
completely unclear to me. I take Chaitin's point to be metaphorical.)


7. Why study AIT rather than other areas?

Because AIT offers a compelling way of looking at things, from ecologies to
economies to the building of complicated objects.

Like I said, it is not the only thing one needs, just a part. Other
interests, such as how to zone refine lunar ores or how to program large
arrays of computers, are probably "meatier" subjects (fully in line with
AIT's predictions, if fact, in the sense that these areas have a lot of
structure, specific bits of knowhow, etc, whereas AIT has a relatively few
"bits" in it...just the foundational ideas.).

I fully concede that there's more day-to-day work that is possible in, say,
genetic programming. A hundred or so researchers are busy testing ideas,
exploring the space of GP and GAs.

So, treat this foray into algorithmic information theory as a glimpse into
a way of looking at things that may make other fields more tractable--just
as Shannon's information theory provided powerful ways of looking at
communications bandwidths, signalling rates, and so on...even while the
actual work of building transmitters and satellites, and launching them,
was in some sense "meatier."

To my mind, the information side of life, self-reproducing systems, and
nanotech, has as much content as the "materials science" and "molecular
machines" side.


I hope this "NanoFAQ" helps. Perhaps it will someday be expanded to a
"MicroFAQ," but don't hold your breath.


-Tim May

