Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 10:04:43 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: AIT VirtSem:  Koppel's "sophistication"

The following paper is interesting and perhaps relevant to what
we're doing:

Moshe Koppel.  Structure.  from Rolf Herken, ed.  _The Universal Turing
Machine: A Half-Century Survey_.  Oxford Science PUblications.  1988.

The minimal description of a string S relative to some Universal Turing
Machine (UTM), as we've seen, is just the shortest input to that UTM
that produces S.  The length of that input is the "complexity" of S.
Simple, cool, and useful.

But not useful enough.

A problem is that random strings have maximal complexity in this
technical sense but don't seem complex to us.  In this seminar,
we've been discussing some ideas about how to take into account
an object's "fitness", which is one part of the puzzle.  I am working
on an essay on this topic to be presented later in the VirtSemester.

Thomas Cover apparently suggested in 1985 that a string's minimal
description actually has two parts -- one describing the string's
structure, and another selecting from among the strings with that
particular structure.  The random string has nothing for the
structure part; pi has little or nothing for the selection part.

Koppel suggests that we can make this intuitively appealing
distinction formal by simply adding another input tape to the
UTM.  Now there are two different tapes -- one for the program
and one for the data that the program operates on (actually, a
bit more complicated than that, but this is the essential point).

The length of the program required to compress the string (compression
measured by the sum of program and data size) within some factor
of optimal is called the string's "sophistication", and is intended
as a measure that matches well with our intuitive ideas of complexity.
In that, it's similar to Bennett's logical depth, though it is a
quite different property.

I think this highlights some of the deep issues in the distinction
between program and data, particularly for self-reproducing systems.
Relating that distinction to the cluster of other conceptual frameworks
we're batting around seems difficult but important.

derek

