Sure, a binary string can encode anything you want.  But for genetic
search to work properly, you need significant closure of "goodness"
under the crossover operator.  Otherwise, crossover is no better
than selecting randomly, and your search technique approximates slow
simulated annealing (slow because you're running an entire
population in parallel, probably on a serial machine -- also, your
mutation operator is probably not tuned for SA).  

Good encodings also can't act pathologically under mutation: a point
change should not drastically change the entire interpretation.
This is easier to get than good crossover, though; you just have to
avoid "compressed" representations.

