Date: Thu, 1 Jul 93 09:01:02 -0700
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

I'm a couple of days behind in my AIT reading, so here is a slightly late
comment.

Traditional information theory measured the information content of a string
in terms of how likely that string was to be produced.  A string which was
a priori unlikely carries much information; one which was likely contains
very little.  Therefore, it was not meaningful to discuss the information
content of a string standing on its own; it only made sense if the string
was part of an environment, a family of possible strings with a probability
calculable for each string.

Chaitin's AIT cuts away the environment, challenging traditional IT by
claiming that strings themselves have information, and offering an
(uncomputable, unfortunately) algorithm for finding out how much information
is in any one string.  This produces a theory which is cleaner and more
universal, IMO.

I am concerned that in the discussion here we are moving away from Chaitin's
modern view towards something more resembling traditional information theory.
The introduction of fitness landscapes, and stipulations that the objects
under study (organisms, or Go programs) have to have a certain amount of 
fitness in competition with others, add a great deal of intellectual baggage
to an otherwise clean theory.

We may agree that in "pure" AIT a blended frog (one whose molecules have been
given random positions) has greater information content (at the molecular
level) than a living frog.  Derek proposes to "fix" this by requiring that
the frog under discussion succeeds in a competitive environment.

But this moves us from a structural discussion to a functional one.  And it
requires a certain amount of human intuition and understanding to recognize
that a frog is competing, that it has an environment, and that it is
engaging in certain kinds of activities (reproduction, etc.).  Granted,
once this is done, it may be possible to define measures of complexity that
are appropriate.  But I feel that much of the motivation of AIT was to
avoid the whole need for this.   

Consider the specific question of recognizing the existence of life.  It seems 
to me that the frog-as-part-of-a-fitness-landscape point of view presupposes
that we recognize that the frog is alive.  Contrast this with Chaitin's
effort to solve the same problem: the notion of mutual information between
separated regions of the frog.  He argues that one region of a living creature
(one cell of a frog, say) can be used to predict the structure of another
region (another cell) with a reduced program size in the AIT sense.

Chaitin's idea may not work (it's criticized in the Santa Fe Institute
complexity proceedings) but at least he is sticking with objective measurements
which can be made without having to figure out what the frog is doing.

Similarly, the suggestion that the complexity of an entity depends on its
environment is out of keeping with the spirit of AIT, in my view.  A frog
would be just as complex in a glass jar on the moon as it would in a pond
on earth.

I think Tim's notion of "critical bits" also requires this addition of an
environment and a functional criterion by which to judge functional
deterioration when bits are changed.

Taking the poker example, AIT will already tell us that four aces has less
randomness than a typical hand.  To try to go beyond this and find a measure
which gave higher complexity to four aces plus a king than to four aces
plus a queen (on the theory that poker hands of higher value should have
greater complexity), based on the fitness of these hands in the poker
environment, is not a useful move, IMO.

In general, I feel that we should avoid as much as possible bringing in
arbitrary, human-recognized measures in judging complexity and randomness.
We should stick to measures which can be formalized, which are objective,
and which do not require an a priori recognition of purpose and function.
Otherwise I fear we are arguing in circles, claiming to have achieved a
new recognition of complexity when we are actually relying on our intuition
about what is and is not complex.

Hal Finney
hfinney@shell.portal.com

